  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Sunday: Scattered Light Showers, Trending Warmer\n\nIsolated to scattered showers continue across portions \nnortheast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin this afternoon associated\nwith an area of 925-850hPa warm air and theta-e advection. \nThese showers should largely stay along and south of the \nMinnesota/Iowa border given drier air present below 10kft to the\nnorth, though can't rule out some sprinkles up to I-90 as we \nhead into the evening. Elsewhere, cloud cover will continue to \nincrease, but dry conditions are expected. Where showers do \ndevelop, moisture is fairly shallow so amounts should only be a \nfew hundreths of an inch.\n\nLight showers increase in coverage overnight as a 700-500hPa \nshortwave trough across Texas lifts northward atop the \npreviously mentioned lower level forcings, coinciding with a \nstronger northward surge of low level moisture. While coverage \nwill still largely be scattered, much of the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley should see some light rainfall through Saturday \nmorning (50-70%), most likely across northeast Iowa and \nsouthwest Wisconsin where deeper moisture remains. Still not \nexpecting a washout, but if some elevated instability noted in \nRAP soundings (100-200 J/kg) can be realized, some rumbles of \nthunder and localized rainfall amounts up to 0.3 inches are \npossible (30-50%).\n\nThe low level forcings move into northeast Wisconsin Saturday \nafternoon and evening, resulting in diminishing showers and some\ndrier trick-or-treating conditions across our area. That said, \ncan't rule out some residual light showers lingering into \nSunday morning as various bits of shortwave energy slowly \nmeander through the Upper Midwest, but mostly dry conditions \nshould briefly return Sunday afternoon and evening. Given \npersistent southerly winds throughout the weekend, expect a \nwarming trend as we head into the new week with high \ntemperatures in the low to mid 60s by Sunday, around 10 to 20 \ndegrees above normal.\n\n\nMonday - Wednesday: Widespread Rainfall, Continued Warmth\n\nHeading into next week, longwave troughing over the western \nUnited States moves eastward into the central Great Plains while\na shortwave trough dives southward out of Canada. These \nfeatures are expected to produce a fairly stout surface low \npressure system that lifts northeastward into and through the \nUpper Midwest. At this point, it looks to be a near certainty \n(80-95%) that we will see rainfall across the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley given substantial QG forcing associated with these\nfeatures. \n\nTo coincide with the forcing, an impressive northward surge of \nmoisture is expected as the low lifts northward. The 30.00z \nNAEFS/EPS suggests PWATs should be above the 99th percentile of \nclimatology, and even indicate they could be above the \nclimatological maximum. That is to say, the atmosphere should \nbe fairly juiced up for some efficient rainfall, but where this \nmoisture ultimately ends up, and by association the greatest \nrainfall amounts, is a bit in question. The majority of the \nensemble guidance (GEFS/EPS/GEPS and their AI equivalents) \ndepict the axis of heaviest rainfall to our west closer to the\ncurrent favored track of the low, but there are some members \nthat trend the low further eastward which would result in higher\nrainfall amounts across our area. The 10th-90th spread of \nrainfall amounts in the 30.13z NBM is large at this point given \nthe uncertainty of the low's track, but probabilities currently \nfavor at least 1/2\" (70-90%) and are fairly high for 1+\" \n(40-60%). Otherwise, above normal temperatures continue through \nmidweek before trending cooler behind the departing low.",
